            <ArticleTitle>[Effects of delayed-action tetracosactide on chronic pain].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>190-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Beta 1-24 corticotrophin was the object of an open trial in the &quot;acute&quot; treatment of chronic pain in 22 patients. The analgesic effect of the drug was assessed before and after two weeks of treatment by means of 3 subjective tests: visual analogue scale, numeric scale and Mac Gill pain questionnaire. The results were statistically significant (P less than 0.01). They confirmed that slow-release tetracosactide exerts a beneficial effect on the sensory-discriminative and affective-emotional components of pain. The drug may be prescribed for short periods (1 to 2 weeks) to patients with chronic pain refractory to the usual treatments. Its analgesic effect is immediate and enables a conventional treatment to be instituted.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
